Yes! While there isn’t a *Linux-specific game that’s designed purely as a teaching tool for driving a manual (stick shift) car, there are a few games/simulators that run on Linux and can help you practice manual transmission concepts (clutch control, shifting gears, stall behavior) in a realistic driving setting. Here are your best options:
🚗 1. BeamNG.drive — Most realistic general simulator for manual driving

BeamNG.drive is a vehicle simulation game available on Linux that uses soft-body physics and has very realistic car handling — including stalling, rev-matching, and manual clutch/shifter interaction with the right controls. Many users report that while it won’t replace real wheel/foot feel, it’s one of the closest virtual experiences you’ll get for learning gear timing, clutch use, and stall behavior before real life practice.

👉 Pros: Realistic physics, supports manual gearboxes, and you can stall the car like in real life.
👉 Cons: Does not provide structured tutorials or teaching lessons — you learn by playing and experimenting.
👉 Linux: Native support (though early/experimental builds can be hit-or-miss).
🕹️ 2. VDrift — Open-source driving simulator with manual control support

VDrift is a free, open-source driving/racing simulator that runs on Linux. While it’s more race-centric than driving-school software, it does let you practice controlling a car’s speed and gear changes and can be a lightweight way to get comfortable with manual transmissions.

👉 Best for: Practicing shifting and throttle control on simple tracks.
🛞 3. Speed Dreams — Open-source racing game with physics

Similar to VDrift, Speed Dreams is a Linux-compatible racing simulation with configurable physics and some manual transmission models. It’s not a teaching game, but it’s useful for practicing gear selection and speed management.
